section.section_3_1
    h1.caption The World That Made Stalin — and the World That Stalin Made
    ul
        li
            strong Author:
            |&#32;Ronald Suny
        li
            strong Published:
            |&#32;
            span(data-type="date") 9 October, 2020
        li
            a(href="") Source
    p
        |Some aspects of
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |life will always remain a mystery. But a fresh look at the Soviet dictator’s formative years can help us understand the rise and fall of the system he built.
    figure.p3_1.style4
        img(src=path + "issues/img/art_3_1.webp")
        figcaption
            span
                span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Joseph Stalin
                |in
                span(data-type="date") 1902
            span
                |Source: Stalin Digital Archive /
                span(data-type="organization" data-id="WiCo") Wikimedia Commons
    p
        |This article is based on the introduction to
        span(data-type="person" data-id="WiSe") Ronald Grigor Suny’s
        |book Stalin: Passage to Revolution (
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="PrUP") Princeton University Press
        |,
        span(data-type="date") 2020
        |).
    p
        |It is the unhappy fate of a historian of the
        span(data-type="place" data-id="USSR") Soviet Union
        |to have to deal with
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |, and the fraught question of how the popular
        span(data-type="event" data-id="InSU") revolution
        |that took place a dozen years before he achieved supreme power mutated from a vast movement for social and political emancipation into a murderous despotism. The Left has been saddled, hobbled, crippled by the legacy of
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |and Stalinism. Liberals and conservatives constantly resurrect the dark years of his rule as proof of the true meaning and nature of socialism.
    p
        |An effort to understand the specificities and complexities of
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plRu") Russia
        |, its
        span(data-type="event" data-id="InSU") revolution
        |and
        span(data-type="event" data-id="RuCW") civil war
        |, and the colossal transformation of a peasant society into an urban and industrial Great Power requires investments of time, energy, and curiosity, combined with much humility about what we know and can be expected to find out. But then that is what the writing of history is all about.